class Solution {
    struct Node {
        array<int, 26> child;
        int count = 0;

        Node() {
            child.fill(-1);
        }
    };

public:
    vector<int> sumPrefixScores(vector<string>& words) {
        vector<Node> nodes;
        nodes.emplace_back(); // Root, index 0

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int u = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                int c = ch - 'a';
                int v = nodes[u].child[c];

                if (v == -1) {
                    v = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());
                    nodes[u].child[c] = v;
                    nodes.emplace_back();
                }

                u = v;
                ++nodes[u].count;
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(words.size());

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int u = 0;
            int score = 0;

            // Every queried word was inserted, so these children exist.
            for (char ch : word) {
                u = nodes[u].child[ch - 'a'];
                score += nodes[u].count;
            }

            answer.push_back(score);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

This assumes lowercase English letters and that node indices and scores fit in `int`, as in the original code.

**What improves:**
- Each node typically occupies **108 bytes**, rather than roughly **240 bytes plus allocator overhead**.
- There is no separate allocation for each node or its children.
- `const string&` avoids copying each word.
- Iteration avoids recursive stack usage.
- All nodes are automatically freed. Your original trie leaks its allocated nodes because it has no destructor.

**Capacity trade-off:** The vector may have unused capacity and temporarily use extra memory during growth. Reserving `1 + sum(word lengths)` avoids growth, but can waste substantial memory when words share prefixes or are duplicates. Use that upper bound only when the trade-off is appropriate.

If memory is still too high, use sparse child storage—for example, pooled linked edges storing only existing transitions—instead of 26 slots per node. This saves more memory at the cost of scanning a node’s children.

