Do you need bytes for **storage/network transmission**, or to pass to a **specific native API**? Those require different formats.

A `HashSet<string>` cannot be copied into a self-contained byte array by raw-memory marshalling: it contains references to other managed objects, and its internal layout is runtime-dependent.

Assuming you want its **contents encoded as bytes**, here is a simple explicit binary format:

- 32-bit little-endian element count.
- For each element: 32-bit little-endian UTF-8 byte length, then those bytes.
- A length of `-1` represents `null`.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

public static class SetEncoding
{
    public static byte[] ToBytes(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        // Reject malformed UTF-16 rather than silently replacing characters.
        var utf8 = new UTF8Encoding(false, true);

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using var writer = new BinaryWriter(stream, utf8, leaveOpen: true);

        writer.Write(set.Count);

        foreach (var value in set)
        {
            if (value == null)
            {
                writer.Write(-1);
                continue;
            }

            byte[] bytes = utf8.GetBytes(value);
            writer.Write(bytes.Length);
            writer.Write(bytes);
        }

        writer.Flush();
        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}
```

```csharp
byte[] bytes = SetEncoding.ToBytes(
    new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana" });
```

This is serialization, not an image of the managed object. It does not preserve the set’s comparer; supply the same comparer when reconstructing it. Enumeration order is unspecified, so sort the elements if you need deterministic output. Do not modify the set while encoding it.

If you must preserve *every possible C# string*, including unpaired UTF-16 surrogates, use an explicitly defined UTF-16 code-unit format instead of this strict UTF-8 format.

