### My answer

This implementation accepts a single event name or an array of names in `trigger`. All extra arguments are passed to every matching subscriber.

```js
class Events {
  constructor() {
    this._subscribers = new Map();
  }

  subscribe(eventName, callback) {
    if (typeof callback !== "function") {
      throw new TypeError("callback must be a function");
    }

    if (!this._subscribers.has(eventName)) {
      this._subscribers.set(eventName, new Set());
    }

    this._subscribers.get(eventName).add(callback);
    return this;
  }

  unsubscribe(eventName, callback) {
    const callbacks = this._subscribers.get(eventName);

    if (callbacks) {
      callbacks.delete(callback);
      if (callbacks.size === 0) {
        this._subscribers.delete(eventName);
      }
    }

    return this;
  }

  trigger(eventNames, ...options) {
    const names = Array.isArray(eventNames) ? eventNames : [eventNames];

    for (const name of names) {
      const callbacks = this._subscribers.get(name);
      if (!callbacks) continue;

      // Changes during dispatch affect subsequent triggers.
      for (const callback of [...callbacks]) {
        callback(...options);
      }
    }

    return this;
  }
}

const events = new Events();
const onMessage = (text, author) => console.log(`${author}: ${text}`);

events.subscribe("message", onMessage);
events.trigger("message", "Hello", "Ada"); // Ada: Hello
events.unsubscribe("message", onMessage);
```

Registering the same callback twice for one event has no additional effect. Unsubscribing requires the same function reference. Callbacks run synchronously; a thrown exception stops the current trigger.

