## My solution

The final length is fixed:
\[
L=(n-1)\bmod k+1.
\]

However, **we cannot keep an arbitrary subsequence of length \(L\)**.

### Which subsequences are possible?

Let the surviving elements have original 0-based indices
\[
p_0<p_1<\cdots<p_{L-1}.
\]

Every gap before, between, or after these elements must contain a multiple of \(k\) deleted elements. An operation cannot cross a surviving element, so it always removes \(k\) elements from one such gap.

Consequently, the surviving indices must satisfy
\[
p_j=j+q_jk,\qquad 0\le q_0\le q_1\le\cdots\le q_{L-1}.
\]

This condition is also sufficient: delete each gap in blocks of \(k\). Before every deletion, at least \(L+k>k\) elements remain, so all these operations are permitted.

### Check a candidate median

For a candidate value \(x\), call an element good if it is at least \(x\).

Because the problem uses the lower median, we need at least
\[
\left\lfloor L/2\right\rfloor+1
\]
good surviving elements.

Process the original array from left to right. Maintain:

- `dp[j]`: the maximum number of good elements in a valid selection of the first `j` survivors, using already processed positions.

An index `i` can only be the survivor numbered `i % k`. Thus, with `c = i % k`, if `c < L`, update:
```cpp
dp[c + 1] = max(dp[c + 1], dp[c] + (a[i] >= x));
```

Scanning indices in order enforces the required ordering. Each position updates only one state, so a feasibility check takes \(O(n)\).

Feasibility is monotonic in \(x\), allowing binary search.

### C++17

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        int n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;

        vector<int> a(n);
        int mx = 0;
        for (int &v : a) {
            cin >> v;
            mx = max(mx, v);
        }

        int len = (n - 1) % k + 1;
        int need = len / 2 + 1;

        const int NEG = -1000000000;
        vector<int> dp(len + 1);

        auto can = [&](int x) {
            fill(dp.begin(), dp.end(), NEG);
            dp[0] = 0;

            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                int c = i % k;
                if (c < len && dp[c] != NEG) {
                    dp[c + 1] = max(
                        dp[c + 1],
                        dp[c] + (a[i] >= x)
                    );
                }
            }

            return dp[len] >= need;
        };

        int lo = 1, hi = mx;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
            if (can(mid)) {
                lo = mid;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        cout << lo << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n\log \max a_i)\) time per test case, with at most 30 feasibility checks, and \(O(n)\) memory.

