## My answer

The minimum cost is:

\[
\boxed{\frac n2 + 2 \times \text{(number of '(' at even positions)}}
\]

**Why:** Let the balance of a prefix be the number of opening brackets minus closing brackets. Each matched pair contributes 1 to the balance across every gap between its endpoints, so the cost equals the sum of prefix balances.

- Every odd-length prefix has an odd, nonnegative balance, hence at least **1**.
- An even-length prefix ending with a fixed `(` has balance at least **2**.
- Other even-length prefixes have balance at least **0**.

These lower bounds are achievable: at each missing odd position, put `(` if the current balance is zero; otherwise put `)`. The balance after every odd position is then 1, and after every even position it is either 2 or 0, depending on its fixed bracket. A valid restoration is guaranteed, so the last fixed bracket must be `)`, ensuring final balance zero.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        int n;
        string s;
        cin >> n >> s;

        long long answer = n / 2;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i += 2) {
            if (s[i] == '(') answer += 2;
        }

        cout << answer << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n)\) time per test case and \(O(1)\) auxiliary space, excluding the input string.

## Evaluation

### Assistant A

The cost identity and the idea of delaying opening brackets are valid, but the implementation has a **critical indexing error**:

```cpp
